import { _ as _export_sfc, o as openBlock, c as createElementBlock, a3 as createStaticVNode } from "./chunks/framework.CGt8wd3h.js";
const __pageData = JSON.parse('{"title":"5. Portable batteries","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"05-portable-batteries.md","filePath":"05-portable-batteries.md"}');
const _sfc_main = { name: "05-portable-batteries.md" };
function _sfc_render(_ctx, _cache, $props, $setup, $data, $options) {
  return openBlock(), createElementBlock("div", null, [..._cache[0] || (_cache[0] = [
    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_5-portable-batteries" tabindex="-1">5. Portable batteries <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-portable-batteries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Portable batteries&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="_5-1-battery-passport-necessity" tabindex="-1">5.1 Battery passport necessity <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-1-battery-passport-necessity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.1 Battery passport necessity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A portable battery does not require a battery passport under Article 77(1). From 18 February 2027, the Article 13(6)(b) QR-code route applies.</p><p>If classification is uncertain, or a battery is repurposed for a different intended use, classification must be resolved before relying on this result. See Section 3.4, “Determine whether a battery passport is required”, and Section 13, “Labelling, QR codes and battery passports”.</p><h2 id="_5-2-product-and-sustainability-requirements" tabindex="-1">5.2 Product and sustainability requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-2-product-and-sustainability-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.2 Product and sustainability requirements&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Effect on portable batteries</th><th>Provision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Restricted substances</td><td>Annex I restrictions apply subject to their conditions and exemptions</td><td>Article 6(1); Annex I</td></tr><tr><td>Performance and durability</td><td>Additional minimum values apply only to portable batteries of general use, excluding button cells, from the conditional date</td><td>Article 9; Annex III</td></tr><tr><td>Removability and replaceability</td><td>Products incorporating portable batteries are subject to end-user removal and replacement rules, express derogations and exclusions</td><td>Article 11(1)–(3), (7)–(8)</td></tr><tr><td>Labelling and information</td><td>General, capacity or minimum-duration labels apply according to battery type; separate-collection, chemical-symbol and QR-code rules also apply</td><td>Article 13(1)–(7); Annex VI</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Article 5 requires compliance with requirements applicable to the battery; it does not make another category’s requirements universal (Article 5(1)–(2)). Common conformity, actor and market-surveillance rules are addressed in Sections 10 and 11.</p><h3 id="_5-2-1-batteries-incorporated-in-products" tabindex="-1">5.2.1 Batteries incorporated in products <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-2-1-batteries-incorporated-in-products" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.2.1 Batteries incorporated in products&quot;">​</a></h3><p>From 18 February 2027, a person placing on the market a product incorporating a portable battery must ensure that the battery is readily removable and replaceable by the end-user throughout the product lifetime (Article 11(1); Article 96(2)(a)). The rule concerns the entire battery, not individual cells or other parts. Readily removable means removable with commercially available tools, without specialised tools unless supplied free with the product, proprietary tools, thermal energy or solvents.</p><p>The product must include instructions and safety information on battery use, removal and replacement, permanently available on a public website in a form easily understood by end-users.</p><p>The rule is subject to Article 11(2)–(3) derogations and exclusions. Replacement batteries must be available as spare parts for at least five years after the last equipment-model unit is placed on the market, at a reasonable and non-discriminatory price; software must not impede replacement with a compatible battery or key components (Article 11(7)–(8)). Current guidance and any Article 11(4) derogation must be checked (OQ-REG-004).</p><p>The battery and host product are separate objects. Master data must state which object each attribute describes and maintain their relationship.</p><h2 id="_5-3-additional-requirements-for-portable-batteries-of-general-use" tabindex="-1">5.3 Additional requirements for portable batteries of general use <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-3-additional-requirements-for-portable-batteries-of-general-use" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.3 Additional requirements for portable batteries of general use&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A portable battery of general use is a portable-battery subcategory. It comprises batteries specifically designed to be interoperable and having one of the formats listed in Article 3(1), point (10): 4.5 V (3R12), button cell, D, C, AA, AAA, AAAA, A23 or 9 V (PP3). The definition covers rechargeable and non-rechargeable batteries. The interoperable design and listed format are cumulative elements; a battery is not within the subcategory merely because it is portable or has a similar use.</p><p>Portable batteries of general use have the same Article 77(1) passport outcome and Article 13(6)(b) QR-code route as other portable batteries. Their material differences are the Article 9 performance and durability requirements and separate reporting under Article 75(1)(a).</p><h3 id="_5-3-1-performance-and-durability" tabindex="-1">5.3.1 Performance and durability <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-3-1-performance-and-durability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.3.1 Performance and durability&quot;">​</a></h3><p>From 18 August 2028 or 24 months after the Article 9(2) delegated act enters into force, whichever is later, manufacturers must ensure that portable batteries of general use other than button cells meet the minimum values established by that act for the applicable Annex III electrochemical performance and durability parameters (Article 9(1)–(2)).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Battery type</th><th>Annex III parameters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-rechargeable</td><td>Average minimum duration, discharge performance, resistance to leakage and, where applicable, delayed discharge performance</td></tr><tr><td>Rechargeable</td><td>Rated capacity, charge retention, capacity recovery and endurance in cycles</td></tr><tr><td>Button cell</td><td>Excluded from the minimum-value requirements in Article 9(1)–(2)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Article 9(2) delegated act is not present in the controlled source set. Its minimum values, measurement conditions and resulting application date must therefore be verified before operational requirements are established.</p><p>Article 9(3) requires the Commission to assess, by 31 December 2030, measures concerning non-rechargeable portable batteries of general use. It does not itself prohibit or phase out those batteries.</p><h3 id="_5-3-2-separate-reporting" tabindex="-1">5.3.2 Separate reporting <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-3-2-separate-reporting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.3.2 Separate reporting&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Producers must separately report the quantity of portable batteries of general use made available for the first time in a Member State, in addition to the reporting applicable to portable batteries generally (Article 75(1)(a)). The first reporting period and format must be confirmed from the implementing act adopted under Article 76(5); see OQ-REG-011.</p><p>The separate reporting requirement does not create a separate collection-rate target for this subcategory. Article 94(2)(e) instead requires the Commission’s review to consider whether such a target should be introduced. Except for Article 9 and the separate reporting identified above, portable batteries of general use remain subject to the portable-battery requirements in this section.</p><h2 id="_5-4-labelling-markings-qr-code-and-conformity" tabindex="-1">5.4 Labelling, markings, QR code and conformity <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-4-labelling-markings-qr-code-and-conformity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.4 Labelling, markings, QR code and conformity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The manufacturer is responsible for Article 13 labels and markings; importers and distributors verify them under Articles 41(2) and 42(2).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Portable-battery result</th><th>Date or trigger</th><th>Provision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General label</td><td>Annex VI, Part A information, including manufacturer, category, Article 38(6) identification, manufacturing data, weight, capacity and chemistry</td><td>18 August 2026 or 18 months after the Article 13(10) act enters into force, whichever is later</td><td>Article 13(1), (7), (10); Annex VI, Part A</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity label</td><td>Rechargeable portable batteries</td><td>Same conditional trigger</td><td>Article 13(2), (7), (10)</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum-duration and “non-rechargeable” label</td><td>Non-rechargeable portable batteries</td><td>Same conditional trigger</td><td>Article 13(3), (7), (10)</td></tr><tr><td>Separate-collection symbol</td><td>Applies, with the packaging alternative for the specified small-size case</td><td>18 August 2025</td><td>Article 13(4), (7); Annex VI, Part B</td></tr><tr><td>Cd or Pb symbol</td><td>Applies above the stated cadmium or lead thresholds</td><td>Transition subject to OQ-REG-002</td><td>Article 13(5), (7)</td></tr><tr><td>QR code</td><td>Access to applicable Article 13(1)–(5) information, the EU declaration, applicable Article 52(3) report and Article 74(1)(a)–(f) information</td><td>18 February 2027</td><td>Article 13(6)(b), (7); Annex VI, Part C</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Where affixing an Article 13 label or QR code to the battery is impossible or unwarranted because of its nature and size, it must be placed on the packaging and accompanying documents (Article 13(7)). This differs from the special packaging rule where the separate-collection symbol would be smaller than 0.47 × 0.47 cm (Article 13(4)). One QR code does not replace other physical markings required by law.</p><p>The QR information must be complete, current and accurate. Where the operator or battery is outside Article 52(3), the Regulation does not clearly state how the QR reference to that report is handled; no report duty should be invented (OQ-REG-003).</p><p>Manufacturers must carry out the applicable conformity assessment, prepare technical documentation and the EU declaration of conformity, and affix CE marking before market placement or putting into service (Articles 17–20 and 38(2)–(4)). CE marking is a conformity mark, not a battery identifier, and QR access to the declaration does not replace physical CE marking. See Section 11.</p><h2 id="_5-5-due-diligence-producer-responsibility-and-waste" tabindex="-1">5.5 Due diligence, producer responsibility and waste <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-5-due-diligence-producer-responsibility-and-waste" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.5 Due diligence, producer responsibility and waste&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Battery due diligence is not triggered by passport necessity. It applies only where the economic operator is within Article 47 and is then governed by Articles 48–52. Its current application date must be verified against Regulation (EU) 2025/1561 because the controlled base Regulation predates that amendment (OQ-REG-009). See Section 14.</p><p>A producer must register in each Member State in which it first makes a portable battery available and may not supply there unless registered; this includes batteries incorporated in products (Article 55(1)–(3) and (12)). A GLN does not replace the authority-issued registration number.</p><p>Producers or appointed producer responsibility organisations must establish territory-wide, free collection arrangements for waste portable batteries (Article 59(1)–(2), (4)–(6)). They must attain and durably maintain collection rates of 63% by 31 December 2027 and 73% by 31 December 2030, calculated under Annex XI (Article 59(3); Annex XI). Later amendments to the method or targets must be checked (OQ-REG-013).</p><p>Distributors must take back qualifying waste portable batteries free of charge and without a new purchase, subject to Article 62(1)–(5). Producers or their organisations provide Article 74(1) end-user information; producers provide model-specific information required by Article 74(2)–(3).</p><p>Annual reporting covers, by chemistry and category, amounts first made available and collected, collection rate and destinations; it includes batteries incorporated in products and separately reports the portable batteries of general use amount (Article 75(1), (4), (7)–(9)). The first period and format require confirmation from the Article 76(5) implementing act (OQ-REG-011). See Sections 15 and 16.</p><p>Following preparation for re-use, preparation for repurposing, repurposing or remanufacturing, the operator placing the battery on the market or putting it into service is treated as manufacturer (Articles 38(11) and 45). Article 13(9) requires new labels and QR-accessible status-change information, but exact non-passport granularity remains unresolved (OQ-REG-007). See Section 17.</p><h2 id="_5-6-gs1-implementation-for-portable-batteries" tabindex="-1">5.6 GS1 implementation for portable batteries <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-6-gs1-implementation-for-portable-batteries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.6 GS1 implementation for portable batteries&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Regulation requires identification, information-access and traceability functions but does not prescribe GS1 standards.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>GS1 support</th><th>Granularity and limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identify a battery trade item</td><td>An implementation may use GTIN. GTIN identifies the battery model or traded configuration</td><td>GTIN alone does not identify an individual battery. Pack level and the battery/host-product distinction must be preserved.</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish a batch</td><td>GTIN plus batch or lot number may associate production and corrective-action records</td><td>Article 38(6) permits several identifying elements; it does not universally require serialisation.</td></tr><tr><td>Identify an instance where needed</td><td>An implementation may use GTIN plus serial number. GTIN plus serial number identifies an individual battery</td><td>Portable batteries have no Article 77 passport duty. Serialisation follows another regulatory function or operational need.</td></tr><tr><td>Provide web-enabled access</td><td>A GS1 Digital Link URI may represent the identifier and connect it to controlled resources</td><td>The implementation must satisfy Article 13 content, placement, currency and availability duties.</td></tr><tr><td>Identify parties and locations</td><td>An implementation may use GLNs. GLNs identify entities, functions and sites</td><td>GLN does not replace an authority-issued identifier.</td></tr><tr><td>Identify logistic units</td><td>An implementation may use SSCC. SSCC identifies a case, pallet or other logistic unit</td><td>SSCC does not identify contained batteries; containment must be recorded.</td></tr><tr><td>Share lifecycle events</td><td>An implementation may use EPCIS. EPCIS captures events concerning batteries, host products, logistic units, parties and locations</td><td>EPCIS does not prove compliance or replace assessment, verification, certification, audit or an authority decision.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Granularity follows the function. High-volume portable batteries will commonly use trade-item/model and batch levels; the QR duty does not itself create individual serialisation. Where a battery is incorporated in or supplied with another trade item, the battery GTIN, host-product GTIN and relationship should be separate. GDSN may support master-data exchange only where relevant attributes and target-market implementation are verified (OQ-GS1-001).</p><p>For portable batteries of general use, the implementation must distinguish the individual battery format, a saleable multipack, any higher packaging level and the production batch. An identifier assigned to a multipack does not identify each battery contained in it.</p><p>Technical sources: GS1 General Specifications, sections 1.3.1, 1.3.1.2, 1.3.1.4, 1.3.3–1.3.5 and 1.4.4; GS1 Digital Link Standard: URI Syntax, sections 4.3–4.11 and 5.1–5.5; GS1 GLN Allocation Rules Standard, sections 2.1–2.3; GS1 EPCIS Standard, version 2.0.1, sections 6 and 7.</p><p>See Section 12, “Battery and grouping identification”; Section 13, “Labelling, QR codes and battery passports”; and Section 19, “Supply-chain traceability and information exchange”.</p><h2 id="_5-7-example-rechargeable-aa-batteries-in-a-consumer-pack" tabindex="-1">5.7 Example: rechargeable AA batteries in a consumer pack <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-7-example-rechargeable-aa-batteries-in-a-consumer-pack" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.7 Example: rechargeable AA batteries in a consumer pack&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rechargeable AA batteries specifically designed to be interoperable are portable batteries of general use under Article 3(1), point (10). They do not require a battery passport under Article 77(1), and the Article 13(6)(b) QR-code route applies from 18 February 2027.</p><p>Because the batteries are rechargeable, the capacity label applies from the conditional date in Article 13(2) and (10), while Article 13(3) does not apply. The batteries become subject to the minimum values for the rechargeable Annex III parameters from the conditional application date in Article 9(1)–(2).</p><p>An implementation may use GTIN for the saleable consumer pack. GTIN identifies that trade item. If an individual battery is also managed as a separate trade item, it may have a separate GTIN. The pack GTIN does not identify each contained battery. GTIN plus batch or lot number may associate the batteries with production and conformity records. A GS1 Digital Link URI may support access to information associated with the identified trade item, but Article 13 governs the legally required content and placement.</p><p>The producer must include the batteries in portable-battery collection and reporting and separately report the amount of portable batteries of general use first made available under Article 75(1)(a).</p><h2 id="_5-8-key-dates" tabindex="-1">5.8 Key dates <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-8-key-dates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.8 Key dates&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Date or trigger</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Provision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>18 August 2024</td><td>Chapter VI economic-operator and conformity framework, subject to underlying dates</td><td>Articles 17–20, 38–46; Article 96(2)(b)</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2025</td><td>Separate-collection symbol and Chapter VIII producer-responsibility rules</td><td>Article 13(4); Article 96(2)(c)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2026</td><td>General and capacity or minimum-duration labels</td><td>Article 13(1)–(3), (10)</td></tr><tr><td>18 February 2027</td><td>Article 13(6)(b) QR code and Article 11 product rules</td><td>Articles 11 and 13(6)(b); Article 96(2)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>31 December 2027</td><td>63% collection-rate target</td><td>Article 59(3)</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2028 or 24 months after the Article 9(2) delegated act enters into force, whichever is later</td><td>General-use portable minimum performance and durability values, excluding button cells</td><td>Article 9(1)–(2)</td></tr><tr><td>31 December 2030</td><td>73% collection-rate target; Commission assessment of non-rechargeable portable batteries of general use</td><td>Articles 9(3) and 59(3)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Conditional dates must be calculated from the relevant act’s entry into force before being treated as operative.</p><h2 id="_5-9-limitations-and-open-questions" tabindex="-1">5.9 Limitations and open questions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-9-limitations-and-open-questions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.9 Limitations and open questions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This section does not reproduce the classification tests in Section 4 or extend Article 9 to every portable battery. Button cells are within the portable-battery-of-general-use definition but excluded from Article 9(1)–(2). The portable-battery collection targets apply to the category collectively; they are not separate targets for portable batteries of general use.</p><p>Current-law checks remain necessary for Article 13 specifications/date (OQ-REG-001), Cd/Pb transition (OQ-REG-002), QR access where no Article 52(3) report exists (OQ-REG-003), Article 11 guidance/derogations (OQ-REG-004), circular-operation QR information (OQ-REG-007), due-diligence date (OQ-REG-009), reporting (OQ-REG-011), collection methodology (OQ-REG-013), GDSN coverage (OQ-GS1-001) and GS1 Digital Link governance (OQ-GS1-003). GS1 standards may support implementation but do not establish compliance.</p>', 54)
  ])]);
}
